<p class="sst-lead">Electron backscatter diffraction (EBSD) maps the grains of a polycrystalline metal &mdash; the same
"is this the same as that?" question as the chips, in a completely different imaging modality.</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>What makes it hard is scale: a single map holds <strong>thousands of grain boundaries</strong>, and there
  are no ground-truth labels to train against.</p>
  <p class="sst-claim">SST answers <em>intrinsically</em> &mdash; no labels, thousands of shapes, a decision in
  seconds &mdash; and, crucially, an <strong>explanation</strong> of what differs.</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p class="sst-note">NIST collaborators on this modality: Adam Creuziger (MML), Gunay Dogan (ITL), and
  Jeanie Schreiber (GMU) on the topological extensions.</p>
</div>
